# DARPA/DoD Hardware Reliability Funding, SBIR Timelines, and EDA Startup Case Studies

**Research Date: March 2026**

---

## Part 1: Active DARPA/DoD Programs Funding Silicon Reliability, Post-Silicon Validation, and Hardware Failure Analysis

### 1.1 DARPA Electronics Resurgence Initiative (ERI) 2.0

The original ERI launched in 2017 with $1.5 billion over five years has evolved into **ERI 2.0**, a planned $3 billion over the next five years. The FY2024 budget spans **32 programs totaling $710 million** across five research areas including security of electronic equipment and networks.

- [DARPA ERI 2.0 – IEEE Xplore](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10607275/)
- [ERI Summit Programs](https://eri-summit.darpa.mil/what-is-eri)

**Key ERI programs directly relevant to testing, reliability, and verification:**

**ASSERT (Advanced Sources for Single-Event Effects Radiation Testing)**
The most directly relevant program to hardware reliability. DARPA MTO issued solicitations for compact alternatives to traditional heavy-ion radiation test facilities, aiming to reduce time from design to radiation-qualified deployment by 10x (5–10 years → 6–12 months). DARPA awarded:
- $18.1M to Northrop Grumman
- $10.6M to RadiaBeam Technologies
- TAU Systems (a Laser Wakefield Accelerator startup) joined the program

Sources: [DARPA ASSERT](https://www.darpa.mil/research/programs/assert-alternative-sources) | [TAU Systems](https://www.tausystems.com/news-center/darpa-project/) | [Military Aerospace](https://www.militaryaerospace.com/computers/article/55016960/northrop-grumman-corp-radiation-hardened-single-event-effects-testing)

**NGMM (Next-Generation Microelectronics Manufacturing)**
Phase 2 will emphasize "automating essential processes" including standardization, fabrication, assembly, and **testing** of diverse R&D designs. DARPA committed $1.4B total. Phase 1 awarded July 2024 to UT Austin's Texas Institute for Electronics.

Sources: [DARPA NGMM](https://www.darpa.mil/research/programs/next-generation-microelectronics) | [DARPA announcement](https://www.darpa.mil/news/2024/next-gen-microelectronics-manufacturing)

**POSH (Posh Open Source Hardware)** — Completed, $35M
Developed validation/verification tools for mathematical inspection of analog and digital IP, including simulation and emulation technologies for proof of functionality and security. Synopsys, Sandia, Stanford, SiFive were major performers. OpenROAD emerged as a direct spinout.

Sources: [DARPA POSH](https://www.darpa.mil/research/programs/posh-open-source-hardware) | [Linux.com](https://www.linux.com/topic/embedded-iot/darpa-drops-35-million-posh-open-source-hardware-project/)

**AISS (Automatic Implementation of Secure Silicon)**
Automates incorporation of security mechanisms into chip design flows addressing four silicon security vulnerabilities: side-channel attacks, hardware Trojans, reverse engineering, and supply chain attacks. Performers: Synopsys, Arm, Boeing, IBM.

Source: [Synopsys/DARPA AISS](https://www.synopsys.com/blogs/chip-design/secure-chipset-design-in-darpa.html)

