## Part 2: Realistic Timeline and Overhead for a 2–5 Person Startup

### 2.1 SBIR Phase I: Proposal to Award Timeline

| Stage | Duration |
|---|---|
| Pre-release (review topics, contact PMs) | 2–4 weeks |
| Proposal writing and submission | 4–6 weeks after open |
| Government review/selection notification | ~90 days after close |
| Contract negotiation and award | 1–3 months post-selection |
| **Total: Topic open → first dollar** | **~6–9 months** |

Phase I contracts are **Firm Fixed Price (FFP)**, typically $250,000–$300,000 over 6–12 months. FFP means no DCAA involvement required at Phase I.

Sources: [DARPA SBIR/STTR Overview](https://www.darpa.mil/work-with-us/communities/small-business/sbir-sttr-overview) | [DARPA SBIR FAQ Dec 2024](https://www.darpa.mil/sites/default/files/attachment/2024-12/darpa-sbir-sttr-contracting-faq.pdf)

### 2.2 Phase II: Timeline and Award Size

Phase II is structured as **Cost Plus Fixed Fee (CPFF)**, up to $1.8M–$1.91M over 24–36 months. There is no automatic right to Phase II — you must separately apply and be selected.

**Timeline from first proposal to Phase II first dollar:** 18–30 months

For comparison, **DARPA Microsystems Exploration (μE)** can award $2M OTA contracts within 120 days of topic announcement — significantly faster.

**Air Force STRATFI/TACFI Bridge Programs:**
For companies that have completed Phase II, AFWERX's STRATFI ($3M–$15M) and TACFI ($375K–$2M) programs bridge the "valley of death" to commercialization.

Sources: [AFVentures STRATFI/TACFI 2024](https://www.afrl.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/3491081/afventures-launches-2024-stratfitacfi-notice-of-opportunity/) | [AFWERX overview](https://afwerx.com/divisions/ventures/stratfi-tacfi/)

### 2.3 Administrative and Compliance Burden

**Phase I (FFP): Minimal burden**
- Standard commercial bookkeeping (QuickBooks) sufficient
- No DCAA involvement required
- 3 milestone payments typical

**Phase II (CPFF): Significant overhead**

Before Phase II award, the government requires a **DCAA Pre-Award Accounting System Survey** evaluating 10 SF1408 requirements including direct/indirect cost segregation, timekeeping, labor distribution, and unallowable cost exclusion.

**Practical overhead estimate for a small company:**
- DCAA-compliant accounting setup: $5,000–$20,000 upfront + $1,000–$3,000/month ongoing
- Tools required: Deltek, JAMIS, or Unanet
- Staff time overhead: 5–15% of billable hours devoted to compliance documentation
- Gross margin cap: CPFF "plus" is typically limited to 7–12% of estimated costs — far below commercial software margins

Sources: [Reliascent](https://www.reliascent.com/blog/SBIR-Awards-and-DCAA-Compliant-Accounting-Systems) | [Team 80](https://team-80.com/blog/navy-sbir-accounting-guide/) | [SBIR Basics](https://sbirbasics.com/2025/10/understand-the-key-differences-when-accounting-for-sbir-phase-i-and-phase-ii-awards/)

### 2.4 Other Transaction Authority (OTA) — Reduces Overhead Substantially

**OTAs bypass the Federal Acquisition Regulation (FAR)**, eliminating DCAA accounting systems, Cost Accounting Standards, and many standard contract clauses:
- Over 60% of 2024 OTA awards went to non-traditional companies (startups without prior DoD contract history)
- Average time-to-award through OTA consortia (e.g., NSTXL): **120 days or less**
- DARPA explicitly uses OT authority for Microsystems Exploration and other programs

**Caveat:** OTAs require at least one of: (a) meaningful involvement of a non-traditional contractor, (b) ≥1/3 cost-sharing by performers, or (c) exceptional circumstances. For a startup with no prior CPFF contracts, condition (a) is easily met.

Sources: [AcqNotes](https://acqnotes.com/acqnote/careerfields/other-transaction-authority-ota) | [NSTXL](https://nstxl.org/otas-in-government-contracting/) | [Inside Government Contracts](https://www.insidegovernmentcontracts.com/2024/09/dod-rolls-out-proposed-changes-to-prototype-ota-regulations/)

### 2.5 Support Ecosystems for Startups

**DARPA's Own Programs:**
- **Embedded Entrepreneurship Initiative (EEI):** Since 2022, EEI helped DARPA performers raise over $1 billion in private investment and launch 21+ new products. New EEI Proposers Day announced February 2025.
- **5 Regional Commercial Accelerators (Aug 2024):** DARPA selected Capital Factory (Austin), CIMIT at MGH, FedTech, SRI International, and Wireless Research Center NC to provide investor access, mentoring, and market mapping. Source: [DARPA announcement](https://www.darpa.mil/news/2024/regional-commercial-accelerators)
- **DARPA Toolbox Initiative:** Pre-negotiated access to EDA tools (Synopsys, SiFive, Verific, Cycuity) for DARPA-funded programs. Source: [DARPA Toolbox SiFive](https://www.darpa.mil/work-with-us/darpa-toolbox-initiative/sifive)

**External Ecosystems:**
- **Dcode:** Federal market accelerator, 300+ companies, 40+ unicorns. Source: [Dcode](https://dcode.co/newsletter/august-2025-the-dcode-debrief/)
- **AFWERX/AFVentures:** Air Force commercial innovation arm managing STRATFI/TACFI
- **SBIR specialists:** Team 80, Science Docs, Redstone GCI specialize in SBIR proposals and compliance for small companies

