## Part 4: IP Ownership, Data Rights, and Key Pitfalls

### 4.1 What a Startup Owns Under SBIR

Under SBIR, **small businesses retain ownership of IP they develop**. The government receives only a limited license for internal government purposes — no commercial rights. Key rules:

- **20-year SBIR data rights protection period** for technical data and computer software
- Government's license is "government purposes only" — cannot give data to competitors or use commercially
- **December 2024 DFARS rule changes** (DFARS Case 2019-D043): contracting officers are now **prohibited** from requiring offerors to relinquish SBIR/STTR data rights — a significant protection for startups

**The upshot:** A company that develops a proprietary failure analysis dataset or post-silicon validation tool under SBIR funding **owns that data** and can commercialize freely, subject to proper marking and 20-year protection window.

Sources: [Martensenip.com](https://www.martensenip.com/blog/2024/november/sbir-vs-sttr-understanding-ip-rights-in-both-pro/) | [DFARS Rule Dec 2024](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/12/18/2024-29226/defense-federal-acquisition-regulation-supplement-small-business-innovation-research-program-data) | [DoD SBIR Data Rights](https://www.defensesbirsttr.mil/Data-Rights/)

### 4.2 What Worked in Successful Transitions

1. **Dual-use technology** from the start — build for both government and commercial use cases simultaneously
2. **IP generated at universities** (EnCharge, OpenROAD) has cleaner commercialization paths than IP generated under direct DARPA company contracts
3. **DARPA Toolbox and EEI** as distribution and commercialization vehicles — far underutilized by most DARPA performers
4. **Phase I FFP** allows low-overhead pilot work to validate a market; treat it as a paid customer discovery engagement, not a pure research grant
5. **Retain SBIR data rights counsel** from day one; the markings regime is procedural but critical and irreversible if missed

### 4.3 Key Failure Modes

**1. The Cost-Plus Trap**
Phase II CPFF contracts lock companies into cost-accounting mindsets incompatible with commercial software pricing. Companies that grow dependent on cost-plus revenue struggle to pivot to SaaS or per-seat models, which require fundamentally different accounting, sales, and margin structures.

**2. Valley of Death — Phase III**
Only ~5% of companies graduate from Phase II to Phase III. Phase III has **no dedicated SBIR funding** — companies must find commercial contracts or DoD program-of-record funding independently. Without a clear Phase III customer lined up during Phase II, companies often exhaust funding just as their product matures.

Sources: [NCBI SBIR Phase III](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11392/) | [Long Capture STRATFI/TACFI](https://www.longcapture.com/blog/the-valley-of-death-and-the-opportunities-stratfi-tacfi-provides-to-fill-this-gap)

**3. Customer Concentration**
Government-only revenue creates existential risk. Budget cycles, spending cuts, and program cancellations can zero revenue overnight. The most resilient companies use government funding to build the product and commercial customers to generate revenue.

**4. EDA License Payment Cliff** (specific risk for this domain)
EDA startups receive subsidized Synopsys/Cadence tool access until a Series A or revenue threshold is hit. Once crossed, full license payments trigger — potentially breaking Series A unit economics.

Source: [Semiconductor Engineering](https://semiengineering.com/startup-challenges-in-a-changing-eda-world/)

**5. IP Marking Failures**
Companies that fail to apply SBIR data rights markings to deliverables can inadvertently grant the government unlimited rights to technical data. This is irreversible.

**6. DARPA PM Turnover**
DARPA PMs rotate every 3–5 years. A company whose contract is tightly aligned to one PM's vision can lose momentum when that PM departs.

